@node Capabilities of the Terminal, Output Functions, A Word about Windows, Core Library
@section Capabilities of the Terminal

When writing a program that uses curses, it is important to know the
capabilities of the terminal currently being used.  For example, not
all terminals can print colors or can be resized.  There are functions
to query the capabilities of the terminal.

@subsection Color Capabilities

Among modern terminals and terminal emulation programs, the widest
variation occurs among their ability to
use color.  To get an idea of a terminal's color capabilities, a few
questions need to be answered.

@itemize
@item
Can the terminal display color?

@item
If so, what are the colors that can be used?

@item
Can those colors be changed?
@end itemize

To answer these questions, first use @code{has-color} to determine
whether a terminal has any color capabilities.  If it does,
@code{colors} returns the number of colors available.  A color
terminal will always have a set of pre-defined colors. The function
@code{can-change-color} indicates if the pre-defined colors can be
reassigned.

With the exception of @code{has-color}, all of the above functions
require that the color capabilities of curses be initialized first by
calling @code{start-color}.

The specifics of using color will be described in greater detail someplace.

@defun  has-color
This function returns @code{#t} if the terminal has any color capabilities,
or @code{#f} otherwise.  Requires @code{initscr}.
@end defun

@defun  start-color
This initializes NCurses's color capabilities.  On monochrome
terminals, calling it does nothing and is harmless.
@code{start-color} will usually return @code{#t}, even if the terminal
is monochrome.  If could possibly return @code{#f} if no memory can be
allocated.  Requires @code{initscr}.
@end defun

@defun  colors
This returns the number of colors available.  Requires @code{initscr}
and @code{start-color}.
@end defun

@defun  can-change-color
This return @code{#t} if the terminal's pre-defined colors can be
modified for this session, or @code{#f} if they cannot be modified.
Requires @code{initscr} and @code{start-color}.
@end defun

@subsection Archaic Capabilities

Some of the functions in this category may be archaic to the average
GNU/Linux user.  Most console programs and terminals are fairly
full-featured and speedy.  But, historically, there have been
terminals that were slow and limited in their functionality.  The
function @command{baudrate} will not be commonly used in programs
targeted for modern consoles and terminal emulaters.  They will be
more useful for dedicated terminals attached by a serial link.

@defun  baudrate
Returns an integer indicating the maximum baudrate that this terminal
can handle, or @code{#f} if it could not be determined.  For modern
terminal emulators and consoles, this will be set to some high fixed
value. Requires @command{initscr}.
@end defun

@node Output Functions, Input Functions, Capabilities of the Terminal, Core Library
@section Output Functions

@subsection Beeping and Flashing

The functions @code{beep} and @code{flash} are used to get the
attention of a program's user.  The function @code{beep} make a
beeping noise, and @code{flash} makes the screen @dfn{flash}, which is
reversing the screen's color for a fraction of a second and then
restoring the original color.

@defun  beep
This routine will request that the teminal make a short beep.  If it
cannot, it requests that the terminal flash.  If either is possible,
it returns @code{#t}.  If neither is possible, it returns @code{#f}.  Requires
@code{initscr}.
@end defun

@defun  flash
This routine will request that the terminal flash.  If it cannot, it
requests that the terminal beep.  If either is possible, it returns
@code{#t}; otherwise, it returns @code{#f}.  Requires @code{initscr}.
@end defun

